1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moritz_Cantor Moritz Benedikt Cantor] (23 August 1829 – 10 April 1920) was a German historian of mathematics.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at Mannheim, Germany. He came from a family that had emigrated to the Netherlands from Portugal, another branch of which had established itself in Russia, where Georg Cantor was born. In his early youth, Moritz Cantor 
2 CONC was not strong enough to go to school, and his parents decided to educate him at home. Later, however, he was admitted to an advanced class of the gymnasium in Mannheim. From there he went to the University of Heidelberg in 1848, 
2 CONC and soon after to the University of Göttingen, where he studied under Gauss and Weber, and where Stern awakened in him a strong interest in historical research.
2 CONT
2 CONT After obtaining his Ph.D. at the University of Heidelberg in 1851, he went to Berlin, where he eagerly followed the lectures of Lejeune-Dirichlet; and upon his return to Heidelberg in 1853, he was appointed privat-docent at the un
2 CONC iversity. In 1863, he was promoted to the position of assistant professor, and in 1877 he became honorary professor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cantor was one of the founders of the Kritische Zeitschrift für Chemie, Physik, und Mathematik. In 1859 he became associated with Schlömilch as editor of the Zeitschrift für Mathematik und Physik, taking charge of the historical a
2 CONC nd literary section. Since 1877, through his efforts, a supplement to the Zeitschrift was published under the separate title of Abhandlungen zur Geschichte der Mathematik.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cantor's inaugural dissertation, "Über ein Weniger Gebräuchliches Coordinaten-System" (1851), gave no indication that the history of exact sciences would soon be enriched by a master work by him. His first important work was "Über
2 CONC  die Einführung Unserer Gegenwärtigen Ziffern in Europa", which he wrote for the Zeitschrift für Mathematik und Physik, 1856, vol. i.
2 CONT
2 CONT His greatest work was Vorlesungen über Geschichte der Mathematik. This comprehensive history of mathematics appeared as follows:
2 CONT * Volume 1 (1880) - From the earliest times until 1200
2 CONT * Volume 2 (1892) - From 1200 to 1668
2 CONT * Volume 3 (1894-1896) - From 1668 to 1758
2 CONT * Volume 4 (1908) (with nine collaborators, Cantor as editor) - From 1759 to 1799 Many historians credit him for founding a new discipline in a field that had hitherto lacked the sound, conscientious, and critical methods of other
2 CONC  fields of history.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * [http://www.royalsoced.org.uk/cms/files/fellows/biographical_index/fells_indexp1.pdf Biographical index of former fellows of the Royal Society of Edinburgh, 1783-2002], pt. 1. A-J, page 162
2 CONT
2 CONT http://histmath-heidelberg.de/heidelberg/cantor-grab.htm
