1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Warburg
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Max Moritz Warburg''' (5 June 1867 – 26 December 1946) was a German banker and scion of the wealthy Warburg family of German bankers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Max Warburg was one of seven children born to Moritz Warburg, the director of the family's Hamburg bank, and his wife Charlotte Oppenheim.
2 CONT
2 CONT His siblings were the art historian and cultural theorist, Abraham Warburg; the chief architect of the Federal Reserve Board of the United States Paul Warburg; Felix; Olga; Fritz; and Louisa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT He apprenticed in Frankfurt, Amsterdam, Paris, and London. From 1910 until 1938, he was director of M. M. Warburg & Co. in Hamburg, Germany. As head of that firm, he advised Kaiser Wilhelm II prior to World War I.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1930s, despite the rise of the Nazi Party, Warburg felt there was hope for the future in Germany and tried to wait out the Nazi crisis. Beginning in 1933 he served on the board of the German Reichsbank under governor Hjalma
2 CONC r Schacht. He sold the bank because the 1935 Nuremberg laws set the framework and campaign of “Aryanization”. He then emigrated to the United States in 1938.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life
2 CONT
2 CONT Max Warburg married Alice Magnus in 1899, and together they had four daughters and a son, Eric Warburg (1900–1990), founder of E.M. Warburg & Co, later known as Warburg Pincus.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warburg_family
