1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The son of Maurice Kranz [1843 – 1920] and Fanny Runkel [1855 – 1918]; married Pauline Masur, the daughter of David Masur and Rosa Goldman. They had one son, Lester Masur Kranz who was killed in a plane crash over 
2 CONC the Pacific Ocean on September 6, 1945 while in the Army during World War II. Both Pauline and Sam died a few years after Lester's tragic death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1930, the Kranzs lived in Manhattan, NYC at 607 West 137th Street. Samuel listed his occupation as a clothing salesman.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Monroe News-Star. Wednesday, January 19, 1949 Page 6
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATHS.
2 CONT
2 CONT SAM KRANZ.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services for Sam Kranz, 67, of 900 North Third street, Monroe, who died in a local hospital late yesterday afternoon after a short illness, was held at the Davis-Lawhead Funeral Home Chapel with Rabbi F. K. Hirsch officiat
2 CONC ing, Wednesday at 3 p.m. Interment followed in the Jewish Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Kranz was the husband of Mrs. [Pauline] Masur Kranz who died September 24, 1948.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by a sister, Mrs. Charles Whitmore of New Orleans and niece, Mrs. Harry Davis, also of New Orleans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pallbearers were: George Miller, Clifton Mize, Murry Hudson, Louis Masur, Jr., Milton Gorn and Sylvian Masur.
2 CONT
2 CONT The generosity of Mr. Kranz was documented in a news article detailing his will:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Monroe Morning World. Sunday February 6, 1949 Page 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Churches, Legion, Other Institutions Given Funds.
2 CONT
2 CONT By H. H. Brinsmade (Of The Morning World Staff)
2 CONT
2 CONT With an evident love of humanity in his soul, Samuel Kranz, who died a few weeks ago, drew up in his own handwriting his last will and testament, dated Dec. 5, 1948 a few days before his death here. Kranz's last few years were dar
2 CONC kened by the death in armed service on an overseas battle field of his only son and soon after his wife became fatally ill and died.
2 CONT
2 CONT But these things did not sour the nature of the man. In his will he devised $28,500, largely to charity and worthwhile institutions.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the thought of his wife's death in mind he led the list with a donation of $10,000 to the Damon Runyon Cancer Foundation. Then with the thought of his son Lester Kranz, who gave his life in the war, he bequeathed $3,000 to th
2 CONC e American Legion post of Monroe as a memorial fund.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then with his mind across the seas in Australia, he recalled that his son was engaged to a Miss Joan Langley in Melbourne, and he asked that she be given $1,000 of his estate's funds.
2 CONT
2 CONT Disclosing his broadly religious mind, he bequeathed $1,000 to Temple B'Nai Israel in Monroe and $500 to each of the following churches: St. Matthew's Catholic, First Presbyterian, First Baptist and First Methodist churches.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ouachita unit of the Crippled Children organization is devised $3,000, the Lighthouse of the Blind in New Orleans is to receive $2,000, and other bequests to relatives and friends are listed as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT To a sister, Mrs. Kranz [Westmore] $5,000; to Carol Davis and Mrs. Henry Davis, both of New Orleans, $2,000 each; to Mrs. Charles Saltzman, Monroe, $500.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furniture and silver owned by him is to be sold and the money derived devoted to charity. Personal effects were to be given to Mrs. Sig Masur and Mrs. Edgar Masur, his sisters-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT He concluded: "It is my wish thatin the event I do not provide in my lifetime for the perpetuation of my father's and mother's graves in New Orleans and those of my wife and son in Monroe, I bequeath a sufficient sum from my estat
2 CONC e for the perpetual care of said graves."
2 CONT
2 CONT The will provided in addition that Jack Masur of Monroe and Solomon Goldman of New Orleans were to be executors of the will without furnishing bond.
