1 NOTE {geni:about_me} According to Robin Lustig on myHertiage In 1962, she published a detailed study in the Leo Baeck Institute Year Book on "The Philippsons, a German-Jewish Family 1775–1933." Their history, as she wrote, "seems typic
2 CONC al of many German Jewish families: the very rapid inhaling of German civilization, the high degree of assimilation, and the abrupt ending."
2 CONT
2 CONT from the Jewish Virtual Library http://www.jewishvirtuallibrary.org/jsource/judaica/ejud_0002_0016_0_15700.html
2 CONT
2 CONT [she]  chose a career as a teacher, from 1930 holding a senior position at the Elisabethschule in Berlin until she was dismissed in 1933. Thereafter, she taught at the schools of the Jewish community. In 1939, she immigrated to Lo
2 CONC ndon, where she engaged in adult education and contributed to various periodicals. In 1962, she published a detailed study in the Leo Baeck Institute Year Book on "The Philippsons, a German-Jewish Family 1775–1933." Their history,
2 CONC  as she wrote, "seems typical of many German Jewish families: the very rapid inhaling of German civilization, the high degree of assimilation, and the abrupt ending."
